now lets say some scammer is putting millions of requests on server 1 so it may crash

so there is uneven distribution of the request.

so to evenly distribute the load between servers, we introduced Load balancer

Load Balancer : it is a networking device or a software application that distributes or balances the traffic/load and distributes the traffic among the servers that are highly available.


why they are used?

-So that servers can be highly available
-high performance
-efficiency(as server utilization become efficient)


Now cons of LB???

lets say we have a LB attached ok so 

Clients(C1,C2,C3,C4) -> LB -> Servers(S1,S2,S3,S4)

-Single point of failure (if LB goes down so it will be a problem)
-Overloaded request
-Limited Scalability

ok but all above mentioned cons can be resolved using the correct load balancer ok

what are characteristics of a LB ?

-Traffic Distribution
-High Availability
-Scalability
-Optimization is possible(optimize resource utilization)
-Health Monitoring becomes easy as we have a single point



Types of LB's :

1.Hardware LB (a real physical device in a data center) but costly devices
2.Software LB's : cheaer than hardware
3.Cloud LB's : service provided by cloud providers like AWS, google cloud, Azure etc.
4.Transport Layer/Layer 4 LB's : It uses IP add and port numbers to distribute the traffic
5.Application Layer/Layer 7 LB's : These work at application layer, which is most close to user level.
6.Global Servers LB's : these are located at different geo locations and they transfer traffic to the nearest or most responsive server


How to perform Load balancing ??
There are various load balancing algo's which various load balancers follows.


There can be 2 types of load balancing algos.
1.Static lb algo
2.Dynamic lb algo


Static : 
-Pre-written algo


Dynamic :
-Dynamic algo evaluates and  take decisions based on real-time situation



ALgos:

Static Load Balancing Algorithm : 
1.Round Robin : we distribute the load in circular manner


benefits :
-it is very simple to implement
-it is fair to do

cons:
-if servers are of different capacity, then also requests are distributed evenly, unequal capacities can lead to unequal performance
-Predictability

Another improvised version of this is 

Weighted Round Robin Algo:

eg we have 50 toys and we have to distribute them between 5 child and they are like

C1 (5 yrs old)
C2 20 yrs
C3 15 Yrs
C4 7 yrs
C5 10 yrs

so ofcoirse they will be having different capacity to hold toys

so distribution will be cyclic only but no. of toys each children is
having that will be different

same for servers.


Benefits :
-Capacity consideration
-Flexibility

Drawbacks:
-its bit complex
-Maintenance is required and is higher



2.Source IP Hash Load Balancing Algo : It distributes the incoming traffic among the set of servers based on the hash value of source IP
address.
So based on the hash value of the source ip addresses it do the load balancing.

benefits:
-Consistency
-Predeictability


Drawbcks:
-Can lead to uneven distribution due to preferences
-Scaling



Dynamic Load Balancing Algorithm :
It is making real-time decisions and evenly distributing the traffic according to the real-time observations

1.Least Connection Method :

request is sent to the server having least number of connection at that time.

pros:
-load is dynamically balanced

cons:
-it forgets/ignores the server capacities

if each req is thightly couples then also issue




2.Least Response Time Load Balancing :
here they keep on monitoring the response time, they keep previous data of each server response time and based on least response time they redirect the requests

-high performance

-but its based on historical data so historical data baisness can happen

3.Resource based Load Balancing :
based on the cpu, memory, network and other things monitoring whichever is suitable they redirect requests

but its overhead is managing all these resources is complex

